TAR(1)                      General Commands Manual                     TAR(1)

NNAAMMEE
       tar - file archiver

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       ttaarr [ key ] f archive [ name ... ]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       _T_a_r saves and restores multiple files on a single archive file.  _T_a_r's
       actions are controlled by the _k_e_y argument.  The _k_e_y is a string of
       characters containing at most one function letter and possibly one or
       more function modifiers.  Other arguments to _t_a_r are file or directory
       names specifying which files to dump or restore.  In all cases,
       appearance of a directory name refers to the files and (recursively)
       subdirectories of that directory.

       This is the file-only tar: there is no tape/rmt support, no multi-
       volume handling, and no append/update.  The archive is an ordinary file
       (given with ff) or standard input/output (ff --).  The old V7 512-byte-
       header format is used.

       The function portion of the key is specified by one of the following
       letters:

       cc       Create a new archive; writing begins at the beginning of the
               archive.

       xx       The named files are extracted from the archive.  If the named
               file matches a directory whose contents had been written onto
               the archive, this directory is (recursively) extracted,
               creating intermediate directories as needed.  The modification
               time is restored (unless mm is given).  If no file argument is
               given, the entire archive is extracted.  A symbolic link is
               made again with _s_y_m_l_i_n_k(2) and a hard link with _l_i_n_k(2) to
               the name it was archived with; ownership is restored.

       tt       The names of the specified files are listed each time they
               occur in the archive.  If no file argument is given, all of the
               names are listed.

       The following characters may be used in addition to the letter which
       selects the function desired.

       ff         _T_a_r uses the next argument as the name of the archive.  If
                 the name is `--', tar writes to standard output or reads from
                 standard input, whichever is appropriate, so _t_a_r can be the
                 head or tail of a pipeline.  This modifier is required.

       vv         Verbose: print the name of each file preceded by the function
                 letter.  With tt, gives more information about each entry.

       ww         Print the action and file name, then wait for confirmation; a
                 word beginning with `y' does the action, anything else skips
                 it.

       oo         On output, suppress the directory-ownership records.

       pp         Restore files to their original modes, ignoring the present
                 umask(2).  Setuid and sticky information will also be
                 restored to the super-user.

       ll         Complain if tar cannot resolve all of the links to the files
                 dumped.  If this is not specified, no error messages are
                 printed.

       hh         Follow symbolic links as if they were normal files or
                 directories.  Normally, tar does not follow symbolic links.

       mm         Do not restore modification times; extracted files carry the
                 time of extraction.

       bb         Use the next argument as the blocking factor (blocks per
                 record).  The default and maximum is 20.  The block size is
                 determined automatically when reading.

       BB         Force input/output blocking to fill each record, so _t_a_r works
                 across a pipe where a single read may return short.  Implied
                 for `ff --' on input.

       ii         Ignore directory checksum errors while reading, instead of
                 aborting.

       CC         If a file name is preceded by --CC, _t_a_r performs a _c_h_d_i_r(2) to
                 that name first, so directories not sharing a close parent
                 can be archived with short relative names, e.g.
                      tar cf a.tar -C /usr include -C /etc passwd

DDIIAAGGNNOOSSTTIICCSS
       Complaints about bad key characters, checksum errors, and read/write
       errors.

BBUUGGSS
       On a mounted ProDOS volume
       only the owner's read and write bits of a mode survive; see
       mount_prodos(8).
       The limit on file-name length is 100 characters.

